<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Cardinal Richelieu</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1585">1585</a>–<a href="/years/1642">1642</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/17th-century">17th century</a> · Also known as Armand Jean du Plessis, Cardinal-Duke of Richelieu</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Cardinal Richelieu was a French clergyman and statesman who was chief minister of Louis XIII from 1624 until his death. He strengthened royal power by ending the political independence of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/huguenots">Huguenots</a> at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/la-rochelle">La Rochelle</a> in 1628 and curbing the nobility, and brought <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> into <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thirty-tyrants">the Thirty</a> Years' War against the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/habsburg-dynasty">Habsburgs</a>. He founded the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/academie-francaise">Académie française</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/company-of-one-hundred-associates">Company of One Hundred Associates</a> for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-france">New France</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/cardinal-richelieu.jpg" alt="Cardinal Richelieu" width="320" height="410" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Philippe de Champaigne · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Champaigne_portrait_richelieu_eb.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
